  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_INVALID_SCOPE_TYPE` | scope-type = infrastructure or interface — module-decomposition not applicable |
    | `H_SCOPE_SPEC_MISSING` | `specs/<scope>/<scope>.spec.md` does not exist — run the scope flow first |
    | `H_INCOMPLETE_SCOPE_SPEC` | Scope spec missing required sections (Vision, Architecture, Golden DX) |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` | Module specs exist + mode verb absent + no Pivot Invalidation List |
    | `H_LOW_CONFIDENCE` | Confidence LOW at decomposition lock or final hierarchy |
    | `H_INVENTORY_INCOMPLETE` | Entered the surface step before every module had a complete closed-world inventory |
    | `H_SYSTEM_FIT_NOT_RUN` | `STEP_1_MODULE_MAP` entered before `STEP_0B_SYSTEM_FIT`'s checklist closed, or "вводим новое" recorded without the specific neighbour invariant/requirement ID it fails (`AX_PARALLEL_MECHANISM_REQUIRES_JUSTIFICATION`) |
    | `H_REFINE_BREAKING_CHANGE_NO_LOG` | `refine-module` removes a public entity / weakens a postcondition without a Decision Log entry (`AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED`) |
    | `H_REFINE_BREAKING_NO_IMPACT_LIST` | breaking `refine-module` without a Pivot Invalidation List enumerating consumer modules (`AX_REFINE_MODULE_PRESERVES_CONTRACTS`) |
    | `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE` | A module rule reference does not resolve to an existing file (and not `deferred`) |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator refused the module boundary, an unsettled product/architecture delta, or the final hierarchy |
    | `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT` | A step's Approval Check fires before the step's own format contract (`READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE`) was read this turn — operator may reject and demand the step redone read-first |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Resolve scope, mode; gate scope-type.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Read `specs/<scope>/<scope>.spec.md` (sole source per `AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE`). Accept a
        structurally complete current V2 scope draft or an already-approved current V2 scope per
        `AX_HANDOFF_TO_MODULE_DECOMPOSITION`. Missing → `H_SCOPE_SPEC_MISSING`; incomplete →
        `H_INCOMPLETE_SCOPE_SPEC`. scope-type must be
        product / library (`AX_SCOPE_TYPE_GATE`, else `H_INVALID_SCOPE_TYPE`). Mode per
        `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT` — initial / add-module / refine-module; the mode picked here
        controls step branching downstream (`AX_DIRECTIVE_MODE`). On `add-module` /
        `refine-module`, run integrityCheck ToolCall below first — an over-budget module
        (`SDD_MODULE_OVERSIZED` → decompose, `SDD_MODULE_SPEC_VERBOSE` → compress) is surfaced to the
        operator BEFORE new work, per `AX_HIERARCHICAL_SPECS`.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="integrityCheck">npx gennady sdd-check --all specs/<scope></ToolCall> Use integrityCheck.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_0B_SYSTEM_FIT">
      <Goal>Reconcile with the existing system before module boundaries lock.</Goal>
      <Action>
        FIRST action this step: run orientation ToolCall below and read its output in
        full: the portal line, the one-hop neighbours with their
        paths, entity/contract NAMES, requirement IDs + titles, the consumers list, and its closing
        question. The scope spec's own Module Map is part of this extract's one-hop
        neighbourhood — a decomposition that duplicates a sibling module's already-declared
        responsibility, or a mechanism a neighbour SCOPE already owns, is exactly what this call
        surfaces before boundaries lock, not after.

        Then close, in THIS message, the fixed checklist — record every answer in the scope spec's
        Decision Log when (a)/(b) lands on "new", inline note otherwise:
        (a) Is the module / entity / mechanism we are about to carve out already an existing kind —
        does the extract already name it (a sibling module, or a neighbour scope's mechanism)?
        (b) If not: which existing module / mechanism the extract names are we EXTENDING instead of
        introducing a new one?
        (c) Which neighbour specs (paths, from the extract) will need work for this decomposition to
        fit the system — each is recorded in the owning dependency/impact section or Pivot Invalidation List?
        (d) Which neighbour requirements (IDs, from the extract) conflict with the boundaries we are
        about to lock, and how is the conflict resolved?
        "Вводим новое" answers (a)/(b) ONLY together with the specific neighbour invariant/requirement
        ID the existing module/mechanism cannot satisfy, plus its own Decision Log entry
        (`AX_PARALLEL_MECHANISM_REQUIRES_JUSTIFICATION`) — else `H_SYSTEM_FIT_NOT_RUN`. On a
        genuinely greenfield project with no neighbours, `sdd-orient` degenerates to its one-line
        "соседей нет" — the checklist still runs, (a) still gets an honest answer, (c)/(d) collapse to
        "н/д"; the step itself is never skipped.

        Continue mechanically when the answers only reuse/extend existing ownership or the project is
        genuinely neighbour-free. When they introduce a parallel module/mechanism or leave a neighbour
        conflict with more than one valid resolution, show one delta decision card and use
        `AskUserQuestion` (`QUESTION_RULE_SLIM`): `REUSE/EXTEND <named existing owner>` versus
        `INTRODUCE <new owner> — existing <requirement/invariant id> cannot satisfy <need>`, plus the
        concrete conflict alternatives when applicable. Stop while waiting; no artifact mutation occurs.
        The selected INTRODUCE branch appends one `<ACR>-DL-N` entry per
        `DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`; a reuse/extend selection updates the boundary and continues.
        Do not use an unconditional STOP as a substitute for this decision route.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="orientation">npx gennady sdd-orient --scope <scope></ToolCall> Inputs <scope>: <scope>=scope resolved in STEP_0. Use orientation.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_MODULE_MAP">
      <Goal>Lock module boundaries; update the scope Module Map.</Goal>
      <Action>
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-map-update.xml")` first — this
        step's Approval Check asked before that read is `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`. Module decomposition
        does not require external research by default (`AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH` — that gate
        lives in the scope flow's Architecture step); but if a decomposition pattern or approach
        proposed below WAS informed by a WebSearch / WebFetch pass this session, persist it per
        `AX_RESEARCH_PERSISTED` — researchManifest ToolCall below,
        filled — BEFORE presenting the boundaries, and link the document in this message; research
        performed and left only in chat is the violation, not the research itself. Propose module
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="researchManifest">npx gennady sdd-new research --scope <scope> --slug <slug></ToolCall> Inputs <scope>, <slug>: <scope>=scope resolved in STEP_0; <slug>=short restricted slug chosen for this STEP_1 decomposition research. Use researchManifest.
        boundaries along `AX_SEPARATION_OF_CONCERNS` (data/domain ↔ UI/presentation ↔
        infrastructure; a shared core only once ≥2 consumers need it) — draw the decomposition as
        a diagram, not a bullet list of names: one tier per axis (data/domain · UI/presentation ·
        infrastructure), each candidate module placed under its axis, as an ASCII block in THIS
        message (rung 2 of `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`; worked shape in
        `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-diagram-ladder.example.md`) — this diagram IS what the
        operator confirms next, not a restatement of it. The operator confirms
        (`AX_MODULE_BOUNDARY_BY_OPERATOR`). **Diagram shown, this message, before the Approval
        Check below** — its absence is the same class of violation as `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT` (asked
        before shown); the operator may reject and demand it. Update
        the scope spec Module Map append-only (`AX_SCOPE_SPEC_MODULE_MAP_OWNERSHIP`), converting the
        same shape to the mermaid `flowchart` grouped by axis (`AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC`) — never a
        diagram invented fresh at write time. `add-module`
        appends only (`AX_APPEND_ONLY_MODULE_ADD`). On `initial` this is the monolith→index migration
        (`AX_SCOPE_STAYS_THIN`): move entity / contract / file-level detail out of the scope spec into
        the new module specs, leaving the scope spec as the thin index (Module Map + composition Golden
        DX). Apply BOTH decomposition triggers while drawing any boundary here — size
        (`AX_HIERARCHICAL_SPECS` budget, held by `sdd-check`) and behavior (an entity owning state, a
        lifecycle, error handling, or user interaction) — and name explicitly, in this message, which
        entities earn their own child spec and which stay a one-line index row. The two rules are one
        seen from two steps: an entity that earns a spec here for owning behavior carries the full
        per-operation DbC contract there (`AX_PORTS_AND_ABSTRACTIONS_DISCIPLINE`, `STEP_4_CONTRACTS_DBC`)
        — the index itself holds only links to child specs plus one row per presentation-only leaf,
        never an inlined contract. Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_ENTITY_INVENTORY">
      <Goal>Closed-world inventory per module.</Goal>
      <Action>
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/entity-inventory-format.xml")` first —
        any delta Ask made before that read is `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`. List every entity
        of the module — the complete world (`AX_CLOSED_WORLD_INVENTORY`); anything an
        execution agent adds beyond it is drift. Interview the operator via
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/interview-protocol.directive.xml")`, extending
        its coverage map with the decomposition topics: «Пропущенная сущность» (what the inventory
        still misses) and «Бесхозная ответственность» (a responsibility no listed entity owns). A
        dispute or tangent raised mid-interview opens a nested frame on the flow's stack — this
        step's main path never silently stalls on it (`AX_STACK_BASED_FLOW`).
        Size the interview to the SCALE confirmed for this invocation
        (`AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH`). Keep evidence separated per `AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE`: entities
        named by the scope spec are facts; entities inferred during the interview stay hypotheses
        until the operator confirms them.
        Errors are first-class inventory, not an afterthought: either one listed entity is the
        explicit errors owner, or the inventory carries a module-level `Ошибки: <механизм> →
        <владелец>` line (mechanism = throw / Result / event / …). A downstream BDD ticket cannot
        require behavior on an error whose owner is absent from this inventory — surface the gap
        here, before approval, not at scaffold time. Once ≥2 entities are relationally connected
        (one references, owns, or is composed of another), draw the ER sketch per
        `ENTITY_INVENTORY_FORMAT` as an ASCII block in THIS message before any delta decision (rung
        3 of `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`; worked shape in
        `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-diagram-ladder.example.md`) — a single entity or a set
        with no real relation states that explicitly instead. **Diagram shown (or explicit
        skip-reason), this message, before any delta Ask and before final Approval** — its absence on a ≥2-related-entity
        inventory is the same class of violation as `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`. If a
        module's inventory outgrows cohesion (too many entities / unrelated responsibility clusters),
        PROPOSE sub-module decomposition here per `AX_HIERARCHICAL_SPECS` — the split is a result of
        weighing real alternatives, not the first typical cut (`AX_DIVERGE_BEFORE_RECOMMEND`); the
        operator decides — do not silently let one module absorb a sub-system. All module
        inventories must be complete before the surface step (else `H_INVENTORY_INCOMPLETE`). Apply
        `AX_MODULE_DELTA_APPROVAL`: ask and STOP only when this inventory exposes a new product or
        architecture fork; otherwise carry the derived inventory into STEP_3 without a no-op approval.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_ENTITY_SURFACE">
      <Goal>Public surface per entity.</Goal>
      <Action>
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/entity-surface-format.xml")` first —
        any delta Ask made before that read is `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`. Define each
        entity's public surface (`AX_ENTITY_SURFACE_COMPLETENESS`), including its `Errors &
        Degradation` field: which errors, by what mechanism (throw / Result / event / …), and who
        owns handling/display — do not leave it generic once the inventory named an owner. No
        consumer → no entity (`AX_CONSUMER_DRIVEN`): an entity without a confirmed v1 consumer is
        dropped or `deferred`. Show each entity as its own compact block in chat (type / operations /
        errors) BEFORE asking for confirmation — a batched approval across several entities in one
        multiSelect/confirmation is fine, but only after every entity in that batch was shown
        individually, never as a table skim. When an entity's Lifecycle is a real state machine (≥3
        distinct states/transitions, not just create→terminate), draw it as an ASCII state diagram
        per `ENTITY_SURFACE_FORMAT` inside that entity's block, BEFORE the confirmation for that
        entity (rung 4 of `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`; worked shape in
        `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-diagram-ladder.example.md`) — a 2-state lifecycle stays
        the one-line bullet. **Diagram shown (or below-threshold, stated), this message, before
        any decision about that entity** — its absence on a ≥3-transition lifecycle is the same class
        of violation as `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`. Apply `AX_MODULE_DELTA_APPROVAL`: only a new public
        behavior/error/ownership fork opens a delta-only Ask and STOP; settled surfaces continue into
        STEP_4, and related unsettled deltas may be batched rather than re-approved per entity.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_CONTRACTS_DBC">
      <Goal>DbC contract per entity.</Goal>
      <Action>
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/dbc-contracts.xml")` first — any
        delta Ask made before that read is `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`. Preconditions /
        postconditions per OPERATION — each operation gets its own pre/post lines, plus a mandatory
        line for what happens when the precondition is violated (e.g. a non-existent id): observable
        error behavior, not silence. A general block at entity/service level is for genuine
        class-level invariants only (what stays true across the whole lifetime, independent of any
        single operation) — do not use it as a substitute for per-operation contracts. Written
        textual and language-agnostic per `AX_CONTRACTS_TEXTUAL_AGNOSTIC` (target-language code
        appears only in usage examples). A Port is introduced ONLY on confirmed
        variability or a test seam (`AX_PORTS_AND_ABSTRACTIONS_DISCIPLINE`) — Port-vs-Service is a
        design choice, so apply `AX_DIVERGE_BEFORE_RECOMMEND` (do not default to Port); when the
        module consciously forgoes a Port, the same per-operation DbC lives directly on the Service /
        Adapter and that choice is recorded in the Decision Log (`AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED`,
        `DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`). This is
        entity-type-agnostic (`AX_PORTS_AND_ABSTRACTIONS_DISCIPLINE`) — a Component that owns state, a
        lifecycle, error handling, or user interaction gets a `### Components` DbC subsection in the
        same per-operation shape; only an entity explicitly marked `Presentation-Only` on its Entity
        Surface skips this block. Each
        abstraction carries its justification (`AX_CONTEXT_TO_CONTRACT`); runtime backing is stated
        per contract (`AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_IN_CONTRACTS`).
        Draw the Port/Adapter/Service dependency graph per `DBC_DEPENDENCY_GRAPH_FORMAT` as an
        ASCII block in THIS message before any contract delta decision (rung 5 of
        `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`; worked shape in
        `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-diagram-ladder.example.md`): nodes = every Port /
        Adapter / Service this module defines, edges = `implements` (Adapter → Port) and `depends
        on` (Service → Port), arrows toward the more stable side. A Service-only module (no
        Port/Adapter at all) states that explicitly instead of a silently missing graph — floor,
        never nothing. **Diagram shown (or explicit Service-only statement), this message, before
        any contract delta Ask and before final Approval** — its absence is the same class of violation as
        `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`.

        Right after the dependency graph, when this module's inventory lists ≥2 abstractions
        (entities and/or Ports/Adapters/Services combined), draw the Call Chain (rung 6 of
        `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`, new per the 2026-08-20 visualization-chain decision, per
        `CALL_CHAIN_FORMAT`): who calls whom, in order, for the module's one main scenario — a
        `sequenceDiagram` ASCII block, OR its table-of-steps equivalent (step / participant /
        action / data) when the scenario is linear — both count equally. A module below the
        ≥2-abstraction threshold states that explicitly instead of a silently missing rung.
        **Call Chain shown (or explicit below-threshold statement), this message, before any
        contract delta Ask and before final Approval** — its absence is the same class of violation as
        `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`.

        On `refine-module` / `pivot`, the Delta rung (composition graph with the new node marked
        plus the ADDED Call Chain steps, per `DELTA_DIAGRAM_FORMAT` in `formats/pivot-formats.xml`)
        is drawn later, at `STEP_6_FINAL_HIERARCHY` alongside the textual delta — not here.

        Close the step with two checks before presenting:
        1. Every module / entity / Port in the contracts carries a confirmed v1 consumer or
           near-term variability (`AX_YAGNI_OVERENGINEERING_GUARD`).
        2. Every public option / flag / policy the scope spec mentions is bound to a concrete
           Port / Adapter contract or explicitly marked `deferred / not consumed in v1` with a
           pointer to the task or version that activates it (`AX_NO_SILENT_OPTION_DROP`).
        3. Every entity from `STEP_2_ENTITY_INVENTORY` resolves one of two ways here: a DbC
           subsection above, or an explicit `Presentation-Only` marker on its Entity Surface
           (`AX_PORTS_AND_ABSTRACTIONS_DISCIPLINE`) — neither present is a spec omission, surfaced
           now, not a silently accepted style choice.
        Apply `AX_MODULE_DELTA_APPROVAL`: an unsettled Port-vs-Service, public contract, or error
        semantics fork opens one delta-only Ask and STOP; deterministic DbC expansion of already-settled
        behavior proceeds to STEP_5 without another approval.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_5_FILE_STRUCTURE">
      <Goal>Design the file structure.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Repo layout; physically split `ports/` (or `contracts/`) from `adapters/` (or `impl/`). No God
        objects, file size ≤ 1500 lines (`AX_MODULARITY_LIMITS`); single grep namespace
        (`AX_NAMESPACE_NAMING`). Apply `AX_MODULE_DELTA_APPROVAL`: ask and STOP only if more than one
        materially different file-ownership architecture remains; otherwise carry the derived layout
        directly into STEP_6.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_6_FINAL_HIERARCHY">
      <Goal>Emit the module spec hierarchy.</Goal>
      <Action>
        On `initial` / `add-module`, for each new module create its skeleton via
        moduleManifest ToolCall below — it never overwrites and prints the
        section manifest (which sections are REQUIRED and what to fill); on `refine-module` the file
        already exists, edit it in place, skip `sdd-new`. Content guidance per
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-spec-structure.xml")` — read it
        before assembling the spec; an Approval Check on the assembled hierarchy asked before that
        read is `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`. Every mermaid diagram this spec carries must already have been
        shown to the operator as ASCII in chat at the step where that shape was decided, per
        `AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC` —
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/diagram-vocabulary.xml")`; a diagram
        appearing here for the first time means the operator never saw the shape they are being
        asked to approve. Assemble
        each `specs/<scope>/<module>/<module>.spec.md` to it; link back to `../../<scope>.spec.md`;
        reconcile the scope Module Map as the exact non-empty member manifest: one explicit relative
        markdown link per canonical `<module>/<module>.spec.md`, no duplicate, missing, ambiguous, or
        undeclared module spec. In `pivot` / `refine-module`, draw the Delta rung
        (`AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`, per `DELTA_DIAGRAM_FORMAT` in
        `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/pivot-formats.xml`) alongside the textual Git delta: the
        composition graph carrying ONLY the changed corner with the new node marked, plus the
        ADDED Call Chain steps — the unchanged part of the module is NOT redrawn. As ASCII in
        chat BEFORE this step's Approval Check, per `AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC`; its absence on a
        `refine-module` / `pivot` round is the same class of violation as `H_ASK_BEFORE_FORMAT`.
        In `pivot` / breaking `refine-module` also
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/pivot-formats.xml")`
        (`AX_REFINE_MODULE_PRESERVES_CONTRACTS`, else `H_REFINE_BREAKING_CHANGE_NO_LOG`). For a breaking
        `refine-module`, derive consumer modules from the reverse edges of the module graph (siblings whose
        `Inter-Module Dependencies` declares `--> this-module`) and emit a Pivot Invalidation List in the module spec — else
        `H_REFINE_BREAKING_NO_IMPACT_LIST`. When a module needs rules beyond the scope-wide
        baseline, fill Module Rules Additions in the Handoff section per `AX_RULE_ACTIVATION`
        (shortlist + considered-skips, operator locks the list; architecture / infra rules get the
        per-axiom walk-through); every referenced rule file must resolve, else
        `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`. Before writing, run the self-check pass per
        `AX_ARTIFACT_STYLE_SELF_CHECK`. Write the ordinary V2 spec directly per
        `AX_SPEC_LIFECYCLE`; Git diff carries the review delta. Record affected neighbours in the
        owning dependency/impact fields rather than a temporary manifest. Then run
        integrityCheck ToolCall below (anchor balance + required sections + links + size budget:
        `SDD_MODULE_OVERSIZED` / `SDD_MODULE_SPEC_VERBOSE`) and resolve or consciously defer any finding
        per `AX_CLOSE_WITH_INTEGRITY_CHECK`. Then hand the approved module set to task scaffolding per
        `AX_HANDOFF_TO_TASK_SCAFFOLDING`.
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/preflight-protocol.directive.xml")` and run
        its lazy active-domain checklist. The semantic review target-set is the parent scope spec plus
        ALL module specs in that scope after decomposition; unchanged module specs are read-only members.

        Whether entered directly or because scaffold exposed a spec inconsistency, load
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/review-lifecycle.directive.xml")` once for this
        complete bundle. It runs structural checks, one fresh independent semantic review,
        reconciliation, and operator approval #1 over the actual spec bytes. A scaffold-discovered
        semantic change invalidates prior ticket work; after approval #1, restart scaffold from its
        artifact preflight. Standalone module work likewise hands the approved bundle to scaffold.
        No nested critic, retained worker, session journal, publication state machine, or hidden
        return token exists.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="moduleManifest">npx gennady sdd-new module --scope <scope> --module <module></ToolCall> Use moduleManifest.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="integrityCheck">npx gennady sdd-check --all specs/<scope></ToolCall> Use integrityCheck.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

